ddle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dba6a25a/img_003_da20c291.png\" alt=\"line segment C E\"></span> ?</p>\n","answerOptions":null,"keys":null,"correct_answer":["30"],"rationale":"<p>The correct answer is 30. In the figure given, since <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dba6a25a/img_004_b736941c.png\" alt=\"side B D\"></span> is parallel to <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dba6a25a/img_005_549a3ebe.png\" alt=\"side A, E\"></span> and both segments are intersected by <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dba6a25a/img_006_9eb2fc2a.png\" alt=\"side C E\"></span>, then angle <span class=\"italic\">BDC</span> and angle <span class=\"italic\">AEC</span> are corresponding angles and therefore congruent. Angle <span class=\"italic\">BCD</span> and angle <span class=\"italic\">ACE</span> are also congruent because they are the same angle. Triangle <span class=\"italic\">BCD</span> and triangle <span class=\"italic\">ACE</span> are similar because if two angles of one triangle are congruent to two angles of another triangle, the triangles are similar. Since triangle <span class=\"italic\">BCD</span> and triangle <span class=\"italic\">ACE</span> are similar, their corresponding sides are proportional. So in triangle <span class=\"italic\">BCD</span> and triangle <span class=\"italic\">ACE</span>, <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dba6a25a/img_007_b736941c.png\" alt=\"side B D\"></span> corresponds to <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dba6a25a/img_008_549a3ebe.png\" alt=\"side A, E\"></span> and <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dba6a25a/img_009_b1e93367.png\" alt=\"side C D\"></span> corresponds to <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dba6a25a/img_010_9eb2fc2a.png\" alt=\"side C E\"></span>. Therefore, <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dba6a25a/img_011_3134a7de.png\" alt=\"the length of side B D over the length of side C D, equals the length of side A, E over the length of side C E\"></span>. Since triangle <span class=\"italic\">BCD</span> is a right triangle, the Pythagorean theorem can be used to give the value of <span class=\"italic\">CD</span>: <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dba6a25a/img_012_cadf178b.png\" alt=\"6 squared, plus 8 squared, equals the length of side C D squared\"></span>. Taking the square root of each side gives <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dba6a25a/img_013_6dda4b84.png\" alt=\"the length of side C D equals 10\"></span>. Substituting the values in the proportion <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dba6a25a/img_014_3134a7de.png\" alt=\"the length of side B D over the length of side C D, equals the length of side A, E over the length of side C E\"></span> yields <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dba6a25a/img_015_72cdb31d.png\" alt=\"6 over 10, equals 18 over the length of C E\"></span>. Multiplying each side by <span class=\"italic\">CE</span>, and then multiplying by <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dba6a25a/img_016_aec4980d.png\" alt=\"10 over 6\"></span> yields <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dba6a25a/img_017_503733fb.png\" alt=\"the length of side C E equal to 30\"></span>. Therefore, the length of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dba6a25a/img_018_9eb2fc2a.png\" alt=\"side C E\"></span> is 30.</p>\n","answer_style":"SPR","section":"Math","image_count":19,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959630,"pPcc":"SAT#S","questionId":"dba6a25a","skill_cd":"S.C.","score_band_range_cd":6,"skill_desc":"Right triangles and trigonometry","createDate":1691007959630,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Geometry and Trigonometry","ibn":"05758-DC","external_id":null,"primary_class_cd":"S","uId":"d960ac42-d74d-4b4f-9c73-411254f6ae3f","difficulty":"H"},"raw_detail":{"item_id":"05758-DC","section":"Math","body":"<div xmlns=\"http://www.imsglobal.org/xsd/apip/apipv1p0/qtiitem/imsqti_v2p1\" class=\"stimulus_reference \">\n        <div class=\"standalone_image \">\n          <div class=\"image-options-wrapper image-wrap-center 47706 tcp-adfee843-3aad-4d98-a4cc-ddf3851a6afe\">\n            <img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"The figure presents triangle A, C E, with horizontal side A, C. Side A, E, is perpendicular to side A, C, such that point E, is above point A. Point B, lies on horizontal side A, C, point D, lies on hypotenuse C E, and line segment B D, is drawn such that it is parallel to side A, E, and forms triangle C D B. Side A, E, is labeled 18, side B C, is labeled 8, side B D, is labeled 6, and a right angle symbol is at point A\" width=\"128\" height=\"104\">\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n","prompt":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">In the figure above, <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"line segment B D\"></span> is parallel to <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"line segment A, E\"></span><span class=\"math_expression \"></span>. What is the length of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"line segment C E\"></span> ?</p>\n","answer":{"style":"SPR","rationale":"<p>The correct answer is 30. In the figure given, since <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"side B D\"></span> is parallel to <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"side A, E\"></span> and both segments are intersected by <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"side C E\"></span>, then angle <span class=\"italic\">BDC</span> and angle <span class=\"italic\">AEC</span> are corresponding angles and therefore congruent. Angle <span class=\"italic\">BCD</span> and angle <span class=\"italic\">ACE</span> are also congruent because they are the same angle. Triangle <span class=\"italic\">BCD</span> and triangle <span class=\"italic\">ACE</span> are similar because if two angles of one triangle are congruent to two angles of another triangle, the triangles are similar. Since triangle <span class=\"italic\">BCD</span> and triangle <span class=\"italic\">ACE</span> are similar, their corresponding sides are proportional. So in triangle <span class=\"italic\">BCD</span> and triangle <span class=\"italic\">ACE</span>, <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"side B D\"></span> corresponds to <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"side A, E\"></span> and <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"side C D\"></span> corresponds to <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"side C E\"></span>. Therefore, <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the length of side B D over the length of side C D, equals the length of side A, E over the length of side C E\"></span>. Since triangle <span class=\"italic\">BCD</span> is a right triangle, the Pythagorean theorem can be used to give the value of <span class=\"italic\">CD</span>: <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"6 squared, plus 8 squared, equals the length of side C D squared\"></span>. Taking the square root of each side gives <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the length of side C D equals 10\"></span>. Substituting the values in the proportion <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the length of side B D over the length of side C D, equals the length of side A, E over the length of side C E\"></span> yields <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"6 over 10, equals 18 over the length of C E\"></span>. Multiplying each side by <span class=\"italic\">CE</span>, and then multiplying by <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"10 over 6\"></span> yields <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the length of side C E equal to 30\"></span>. Therefore, the length of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"side C E\"></span> is 30.</p>\n"}},"createDate":1691007959630,"updateDate":1691007959630,"correct_answer_extracted_from_rationale":true}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959630, 1691007959630),
    ($SATQ$dc71597b$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$032655-DC$SATQ$, $SATQ$disclosed$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Geometry and Trigonometry$SATQ$, $SATQ$S$SATQ$, $SATQ$S.A.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Area and volume$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p class="stem_paragraph ">A right circular cone has a volume of <span class="math_expression "><span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dc71597b/img_003_9788e124.png" alt="one third, pi "></span></span>&nbsp;cubic feet and a height of 9&nbsp;feet. What is the radius, in feet, of the base of the cone?</p>
$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a","content":"<p><span class=\"choice_cell align:center \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dc71597b/img_000_7445a232.png\" alt=\"one third\"></span></span></span></p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p><span class=\"choice_cell align:center \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dc71597b/img_001_299e3eb8.png\" alt=\"the fraction 1 over the square root of 3, end fraction\"></span></span></span></p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p><span class=\"choice_cell align:center \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dc71597b/img_002_61b3eb60.png\" alt=\"the square root of 3\"></span></span></span></p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p>3</p>\n"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, NULL, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is correct. The equation for the volume of a right circular cone is <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dc71597b/img_004_9e1f5885.png" alt="V equals, one third pi r squared times h"></span>. It&rsquo;s given that the volume of the right circular cone is <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dc71597b/img_005_9788e124.png" alt="one third pi "></span> cubic feet and the height is 9 feet. Substituting these values for <span class="italic">V</span> and <span class="italic">h</span>, respectively, gives <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dc71597b/img_006_3b7fbb37.png" alt="one third pi equals, one third pi r squared, times 9"></span>. Dividing both sides of the equation by <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dc71597b/img_007_9788e124.png" alt="one third pi "></span> gives <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dc71597b/img_008_00b21f3a.png" alt="1 equals, r squared times 9"></span>. Dividing both sides of the equation by 9 gives <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dc71597b/img_009_48fe4c3d.png" alt="one ninth equals r squared"></span>. Taking the square root of both sides results in two possible values for the radius, <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dc71597b/img_010_9aa35956.png" alt="the square root of one ninth "></span> or <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dc71597b/img_011_5fd94c7e.png" alt="the negative of the square root of one ninth"></span>. Since the radius can&rsquo;t have a negative value, that leaves <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dc71597b/img_012_9aa35956.png" alt="the square root of one ninth "></span> as the only possibility. Applying the quotient property of square roots, <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dc71597b/img_013_66a2e52c.png" alt="the square root of the fraction a, over b, equals, the fraction the square root of a, over the square root of b"></span>, results in <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dc71597b/img_014_e4c9226a.png" alt="r equals, the fraction the square root of 1 over the square root of 9"></span>, or <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dc71597b/img_015_adf5eada.png" alt="r equals one third"></span>.<p>Choices B and C are incorrect and may result from incorrectly evaluating <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dc71597b/img_016_9aa35956.png" alt="the square root of one ninth"></span>. Choice D is incorrect and may result from solving <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dc71597b/img_017_370807a2.png" alt="r squared equals 9 "></span> instead of <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dc71597b/img_018_66d84cbe.png" alt="r squared equals one ninth"></span>.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>
$SATQ$, false, 19, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$Multiple Choice$SATQ$, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ${"questionId":"dc71597b","external_id":null,"disclosed_item_id":"032655-DC","source":"disclosed","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"S","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill_cd":"S.A.","skill_desc":"Area and volume","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">A right circular cone has a volume of <span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dc71597b/img_003_9788e124.png\" alt=\"one third, pi \"></span></span>&nbsp;cubic feet and a height of 9&nbsp;feet. What is the radius, in feet, of the base of the cone?</p>\n","answerOptions":[{"id":"a","content":"<p><span class=\"choice_cell align:center \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dc71597b/img_000_7445a232.png\" alt=\"one third\"></span></span></span></p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p><span class=\"choice_cell align:center \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dc71597b/img_001_299e3eb8.png\" alt=\"the fraction 1 over the square root of 3, end fraction\"></span></span></span></p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p><span class=\"choice_cell align:center \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dc71597b/img_002_61b3eb60.png\" alt=\"the square root of 3\"></span></span></span></p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p>3</p>\n"}],"keys":null,"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is correct. The equation for the volume of a right circular cone is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dc71597b/img_004_9e1f5885.png\" alt=\"V equals, one third pi r squared times h\"></span>. It&rsquo;s given that the volume of the right circular cone is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dc71597b/img_005_9788e124.png\" alt=\"one third pi \"></span> cubic feet and the height is 9 feet. Substituting these values for <span class=\"italic\">V</span> and <span class=\"italic\">h</span>, respectively, gives <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dc71597b/img_006_3b7fbb37.png\" alt=\"one third pi equals, one third pi r squared, times 9\"></span>. Dividing both sides of the equation by <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dc71597b/img_007_9788e124.png\" alt=\"one third pi \"></span> gives <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dc71597b/img_008_00b21f3a.png\" alt=\"1 equals, r squared times 9\"></span>. Dividing both sides of the equation by 9 gives <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dc71597b/img_009_48fe4c3d.png\" alt=\"one ninth equals r squared\"></span>. Taking the square root of both sides results in two possible values for the radius, <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dc71597b/img_010_9aa35956.png\" alt=\"the square root of one ninth \"></span> or <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dc71597b/img_011_5fd94c7e.png\" alt=\"the negative of the square root of one ninth\"></span>. Since the radius can&rsquo;t have a negative value, that leaves <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dc71597b/img_012_9aa35956.png\" alt=\"the square root of one ninth \"></span> as the only possibility. Applying the quotient property of square roots, <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dc71597b/img_013_66a2e52c.png\" alt=\"the square root of the fraction a, over b, equals, the fraction the square root of a, over the square root of b\"></span>, results in <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dc71597b/img_014_e4c9226a.png\" alt=\"r equals, the fraction the square root of 1 over the square root of 9\"></span>, or <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dc71597b/img_015_adf5eada.png\" alt=\"r equals one third\"></span>.<p>Choices B and C are incorrect and may result from incorrectly evaluating <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dc71597b/img_016_9aa35956.png\" alt=\"the square root of one ninth\"></span>. Choice D is incorrect and may result from solving <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dc71597b/img_017_370807a2.png\" alt=\"r squared equals 9 \"></span> instead of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/dc71597b/img_018_66d84cbe.png\" alt=\"r squared equals one ninth\"></span>.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>\n","answer_style":"Multiple Choice","section":"Math","image_count":19,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959627,"pPcc":"SAT#S","questionId":"dc71597b","skill_cd":"S.A.","score_band_range_cd":6,"skill_desc":"Area and volume","createDate":1691007959627,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Geometry and Trigonometry","ibn":"032655-DC","external_id":null,"primary_class_cd":"S","uId":"8936ba3a-68ed-4a79-b894-5d790adff80e","difficulty":"H"},"raw_detail":{"item_id":"032655-DC","section":"Math","prompt":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">A right circular cone has a volume of <span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"one third, pi \"></span></span>&nbsp;cubic feet and a height of 9&nbsp;feet. What is the radius, in feet, of the base of the cone?</p>\n","answer":{"style":"Multiple Choice","choices":{"a":{"body":"<p><span class=\"choice_cell align:center \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"one third\"></span></span></span></p>\n"},"b":{"body":"<p><span class=\"choice_cell align:center \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the fraction 1 over the square root of 3, end fraction\"></span></span></span></p>\n"},"c":{"body":"<p><span class=\"choice_cell align:center \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the square root of 3\"></span></span></span></p>\n"},"d":{"body":"<p>3</p>\n"}},"correct_choice":"a","rationale":"<p>Choice A is correct. The equation for the volume of a right circular cone is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"V equals, one third pi r squared times h\"></span>. It&rsquo;s given that the volume of the right circular cone is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"one third pi \"></span> cubic feet and the height is 9 feet. Substituting these values for <span class=\"italic\">V</span> and <span class=\"italic\">h</span>, respectively, gives <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"one third pi equals, one third pi r squared, times 9\"></span>. Dividing both sides of the equation by <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"one third pi \"></span> gives <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"1 equals, r squared times 9\"></span>. Dividing both sides of the equation by 9 gives <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"one ninth equals r squared\"></span>. Taking the square root of both sides results in two possible values for the radius, <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the square root of one ninth \"></span> or <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the negative of the square root of one ninth\"></span>. Since the radius can&rsquo;t have a negative value, that leaves <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the square root of one ninth \"></span> as the only possibility. Applying the quotient property of square roots, <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the square root of the fraction a, over b, equals, the fraction the square root of a, over the square root of b\"></span>, results in <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"r equals, the fraction the square root of 1 over the square root of 9\"></span>, or <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"r equals one third\"></span>.<p>Choices B and C are incorrect and may result from incorrectly evaluating <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the square root of one ninth\"></span>. Choice D is incorrect and may result from solving <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"r squared equals 9 \"></span> instead of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"r squared equals one ninth\"></span>.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>\n"}},"createDate":1691007959627,"updateDate":1691007959627}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959627, 1691007959627),
