/svg>\n<div role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Long description for right triangle\" class=\"sr-only\"><ul>\n<li>One angle is a right angle.</li>\n<li>The length of the side opposite the right angle is 21.</li>\n<li>The length of one side adjacent to the right angle is 6.</li>\n<li>The length of the other side adjacent to the right angle is a.</li>\n<li>A note indicates the figure is not drawn to scale.</li>\n</ul></div></figure></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">For the triangle shown, which expression represents the value of <math alttext=\"a\"><mi>a</mi>\n</math>?</p>","externalid":"137892dd-a025-491a-a463-42740b162c96","templateid":"1100cd28-ac25-4c83-a2ba-8556ed194144","vaultid":"6c76d345-8cd2-464a-abeb-0d9c6e10980f","type":"mcq","answerOptions":[{"id":"c54aeb75-8ad8-4cd2-837e-4dfc051313f8","content":"<p><math alttext=\"StartRoot 21 squared minus 6 squared EndRoot\"><msqrt><msup><mrow><mn>21</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><msup><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></msqrt></math></p>"},{"id":"073aebc6-405f-4a12-9e4b-7b41a32b8a1e","content":"<p><math alttext=\"21 squared minus 6 squared\"><msup><mrow><mn>21</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><msup><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p>"},{"id":"f3dec4a3-0855-425d-932f-71cb1585083f","content":"<p><math alttext=\"StartRoot 21 minus 6 EndRoot\"><msqrt><mrow><mn>21</mn></mrow><mo>-</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></msqrt></math></p>"},{"id":"f2022250-a3f0-4449-98c5-c013f3a7ab55","content":"<p><math alttext=\"21 minus 6\"><mrow><mn>21</mn></mrow><mo>-</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></math></p>"}],"correct_answer":["A"]},"createDate":1691007959840,"updateDate":1691007959840}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959840, 1691007959840),
    ($SATQ$deff8a2f$SATQ$, $SATQ$1f5958dc-ffc1-426c-8358-d8fbb4354de4$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Geometry and Trigonometry$SATQ$, $SATQ$S$SATQ$, $SATQ$S.A.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Area and volume$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p>The circumference of the base of a right circular cylinder is <em><math alttext="20 pi"><mrow><mn>20</mn></mrow><mi>&#960;</mi></math>&nbsp;</em>meters, and the height of the cylinder is <math alttext="6"><mn>6</mn>
</math> meters. What is the volume, in cubic meters, of the cylinder?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"62696ffb-d01c-438c-bb4f-22182c756e6c","content":"<p><em><math alttext=\"60 pi\"><mrow><mn>60</mn></mrow><mi>&#960;</mi></math></em></p>"},{"id":"6eb29407-9748-49b3-b84f-c1dc9e85a662","content":"<p><em><math alttext=\"120 pi\"><mrow><mn>120</mn></mrow><mi>&#960;</mi></math></em></p>"},{"id":"46e04b54-9eac-4312-9bdd-42369d205924","content":"<p><em><math alttext=\"600 pi\"><mrow><mn>600</mn></mrow><mi>&#960;</mi></math></em></p>"},{"id":"bdbbc586-7fa7-4069-a559-aebc76f9e881","content":"<p><em><math alttext=\"2,400 pi\"><mrow><mn>2,400</mn></mrow><mi>&#960;</mi></math></em></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["46e04b54-9eac-4312-9bdd-42369d205924"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">Choice C is correct. The volume, <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper V"><mi>V</mi></math>, of a right circular cylinder is given by the formula <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper V equals pi r squared h"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>h</mi></math>, where <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="r"><mi>r</mi></math> is the radius of the base of the cylinder and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="h"><mi>h</mi></math> is the height of the cylinder. It&rsquo;s given that a right circular cylinder has a height of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="6"><mn>6</mn></math> meters. Therefore, <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="h equals 6"><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></math>. It's also given that the right circular cylinder has a base with a circumference of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="20 pi"><mn>20</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></math> meters. The circumference, <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper C"><mi>C</mi></math>, of a circle is given by <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper C equals 2 pi r"><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mi>r</mi></math>, where <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="r"><mi>r</mi></math> is the radius of the circle. Substituting <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="20 pi"><mn>20</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></math> for <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper C"><mi>C</mi></math> in the formula <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper C equals 2 pi r"><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mi>r</mi></math> yields <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="20 pi equals 2 pi r"><mn>20</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mi>r</mi></math>. Dividing each side of this equation by <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="2 pi"><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></math> yields <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="10 equals r"><mn>10</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></math>. Substituting <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="10"><mn>10</mn></math> for <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="r"><mi>r</mi></math> and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="6"><mn>6</mn></math> for <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="h"><mi>h</mi></math> in the formula <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper V equals pi r squared h"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>h</mi></math> yields <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper V equals pi left parenthesis 10 right parenthesis squared left parenthesis 6 right parenthesis"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mfenced><mn>6</mn></mfenced></math>, or <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper V equals 600 pi"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>600</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></math>. Therefore, the volume, in cubic meters, of the cylinder is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="600 pi"><mn>600</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></math>.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice B is incorrect. This is the lateral surface area, not the volume, of the cylinder.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice D is incorrect. This is the result of using the diameter, not the radius, for the value of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="r"><mi>r</mi></math> in the formula <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper V equals pi r squared h"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>h</mi></math>.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"deff8a2f","external_id":"1f5958dc-ffc1-426c-8358-d8fbb4354de4","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":null,"uId":"14f001eb-da67-431b-97c9-32238c677dcc","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"S","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill_cd":"S.A.","skill_desc":"Area and volume","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p>The circumference of the base of a right circular cylinder is <em><math alttext=\"20 pi\"><mrow><mn>20</mn></mrow><mi>&#960;</mi></math>&nbsp;</em>meters, and the height of the cylinder is <math alttext=\"6\"><mn>6</mn>\n</math> meters. What is the volume, in cubic meters, of the cylinder?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"62696ffb-d01c-438c-bb4f-22182c756e6c","content":"<p><em><math alttext=\"60 pi\"><mrow><mn>60</mn></mrow><mi>&#960;</mi></math></em></p>"},{"id":"6eb29407-9748-49b3-b84f-c1dc9e85a662","content":"<p><em><math alttext=\"120 pi\"><mrow><mn>120</mn></mrow><mi>&#960;</mi></math></em></p>"},{"id":"46e04b54-9eac-4312-9bdd-42369d205924","content":"<p><em><math alttext=\"600 pi\"><mrow><mn>600</mn></mrow><mi>&#960;</mi></math></em></p>"},{"id":"bdbbc586-7fa7-4069-a559-aebc76f9e881","content":"<p><em><math alttext=\"2,400 pi\"><mrow><mn>2,400</mn></mrow><mi>&#960;</mi></math></em></p>"}],"keys":["46e04b54-9eac-4312-9bdd-42369d205924"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice C is correct. The volume, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper V\"><mi>V</mi></math>, of a right circular cylinder is given by the formula <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper V equals pi r squared h\"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>h</mi></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"r\"><mi>r</mi></math> is the radius of the base of the cylinder and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"h\"><mi>h</mi></math> is the height of the cylinder. It&rsquo;s given that a right circular cylinder has a height of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"6\"><mn>6</mn></math> meters. Therefore, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"h equals 6\"><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></math>. It's also given that the right circular cylinder has a base with a circumference of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"20 pi\"><mn>20</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></math> meters. The circumference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper C\"><mi>C</mi></math>, of a circle is given by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper C equals 2 pi r\"><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mi>r</mi></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"r\"><mi>r</mi></math> is the radius of the circle. Substituting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"20 pi\"><mn>20</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper C\"><mi>C</mi></math> in the formula <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper C equals 2 pi r\"><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mi>r</mi></math> yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"20 pi equals 2 pi r\"><mn>20</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mi>r</mi></math>. Dividing each side of this equation by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"2 pi\"><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></math> yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"10 equals r\"><mn>10</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></math>. Substituting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"10\"><mn>10</mn></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"r\"><mi>r</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"6\"><mn>6</mn></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"h\"><mi>h</mi></math> in the formula <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper V equals pi r squared h\"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>h</mi></math> yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper V equals pi left parenthesis 10 right parenthesis squared left parenthesis 6 right parenthesis\"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mfenced><mn>6</mn></mfenced></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper V equals 600 pi\"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>600</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></math>. Therefore, the volume, in cubic meters, of the cylinder is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"600 pi\"><mn>600</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice B is incorrect. This is the lateral surface area, not the volume, of the cylinder.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice D is incorrect. This is the result of using the diameter, not the radius, for the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"r\"><mi>r</mi></math> in the formula <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper V equals pi r squared h\"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msu