/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"r\"><mi>r</mi></math> is the radius of the base of the cylinder and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"h\"><mi>h</mi></math> is the height of the cylinder. It&rsquo;s given that a right circular cylinder has a height of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"6\"><mn>6</mn></math> meters. Therefore, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"h equals 6\"><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></math>. It's also given that the right circular cylinder has a base with a circumference of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"20 pi\"><mn>20</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></math> meters. The circumference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper C\"><mi>C</mi></math>, of a circle is given by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper C equals 2 pi r\"><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mi>r</mi></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"r\"><mi>r</mi></math> is the radius of the circle. Substituting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"20 pi\"><mn>20</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper C\"><mi>C</mi></math> in the formula <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper C equals 2 pi r\"><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mi>r</mi></math> yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"20 pi equals 2 pi r\"><mn>20</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mi>r</mi></math>. Dividing each side of this equation by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"2 pi\"><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></math> yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"10 equals r\"><mn>10</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></math>. Substituting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"10\"><mn>10</mn></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"r\"><mi>r</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"6\"><mn>6</mn></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"h\"><mi>h</mi></math> in the formula <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper V equals pi r squared h\"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>h</mi></math> yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper V equals pi left parenthesis 10 right parenthesis squared left parenthesis 6 right parenthesis\"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mfenced><mn>6</mn></mfenced></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper V equals 600 pi\"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>600</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></math>. Therefore, the volume, in cubic meters, of the cylinder is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"600 pi\"><mn>600</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice B is incorrect. This is the lateral surface area, not the volume, of the cylinder.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice D is incorrect. This is the result of using the diameter, not the radius, for the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"r\"><mi>r</mi></math> in the formula <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper V equals pi r squared h\"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>h</mi></math>.</p>","externalid":"1f5958dc-ffc1-426c-8358-d8fbb4354de4","correct_answer":["C"]},"createDate":1755117017716,"updateDate":1755117017716}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117017716, 1755117017716),
